Advanced Network Configuration
Creating Custom Network Policies
Step 1: Create a network policy to control VM traffic

cat > vm-network-policy.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: vm-network-policy
  namespace: vm-networking-lab
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      kubevirt.io/vm: basic-vm
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  - Egress
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubevirt.io/vm: multihomed-vm
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 22
  egress:
  - to: []
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 53
    - protocol: UDP
      port: 53
  - to: []
    ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 443
EOF
Step 2: Apply the network policy

oc apply -f vm-network-policy.yaml
Step 3: Test the network policy

# Test that the policy allows SSH from multihomed-vm to basic-vm
echo "Testing SSH connectivity (should work):"
oc exec $MULTIHOMED_VM_POD -- nc -zv $BASIC_VM_IP 22

# Test from debug pod (should be blocked)
echo "Testing SSH from debug pod (should be blocked):"
oc exec network-debug -- timeout 5 nc -zv $BASIC_VM_IP 22 || echo "Connection blocked as expected"
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: VM Cannot Start Due to Network Configuration
Symptoms: VM stays in "Pending" state with network-related errors

Solution Steps:

# Check VM events
oc describe vm <vm-name>

# Check VMI events
oc describe vmi <vm-name>

# Check pod events
oc describe pod <vm-pod-name>

# Verify NAD exists and is correct
oc get network-attachment-definitions
oc describe network-attachment-definition <nad-name>
Issue 2: VM Has No Network Connectivity
Symptoms: VM starts but cannot reach other pods or external networks

Solution Steps:

# Check VM network interfaces
oc exec <vm-pod> -- ip addr show

# Check routing table
oc exec <vm-pod> -- ip route show

# Check DNS configuration
oc exec <vm-pod> -- cat /etc/resolv.conf

# Test basic connectivity
oc exec <vm-pod> -- ping 8.8.8.8
Issue 3: Multus Network Not Working
Symptoms: Additional network interfaces not appearing in VM

Solution Steps:

# Verify Multus is running
oc get pods -n openshift-multus

# Check NAD configuration
oc get network-attachment-definitions -o yaml

# Verify VM annotations
oc get vmi <vm-name> -o yaml | grep -A 5 annotations

# Check for CNI errors in logs
oc logs -n openshift-multus -l app=multus
